<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercises xml:id="exercises-4-3">
  <title>Exercises for Section 4.3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Use Laplace transforms to solve each of the following initial value problems,
      following the three steps: transform, solve for the transformed function, then invert.
      Several of the inverse transforms you will need were already computed in the
      exercises for Section 4.2, and you may use those results directly!
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q1: Homogeneous, distinct real roots
       (inverse transform done in ex-4-2-1)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-4-3-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Laplace transforms to solve the following initial value problem.
        <me>y'' + y' - 20y = 0, \qquad y(0) = 1, \quad y'(0) = 22</me>
        After solving for <m>Y(s)</m> and factoring the denominator, you may use your
        result from <xref ref="ex-4-2-1"/> to finish the problem.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Transforming and solving gives
        <m>Y(s) = \dfrac{s+23}{s^2+s-20} = \dfrac{s+23}{(s-4)(s+5)}</m>,
        which is exactly <xref ref="ex-4-2-1"/>. Therefore
        <me>y(t) = 3e^{4t} - 2e^{-5t}.</me>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q2: Forced oscillator, zero ICs
       (inverse transform done in ex-4-2-4)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-4-3-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Laplace transforms to solve the following initial value problem.
        <me>x'' + 16x = \sin(7t), \qquad x(0) = 0, \quad x'(0) = 0</me>
        After solving for <m>X(s)</m>, you may use your result from
        <xref ref="ex-4-2-4"/> to finish the problem.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        The right-hand side is not zero this time: apply
        <xref ref="formula-D" text="custom">Formula D</xref> to transform <m>\sin(7t)</m>.
        Since both initial conditions are zero, the derivative formulas simplify nicely.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Transforming gives <m>(s^2+16)X(s) = \dfrac{7}{s^2+49}</m>, so
        <m>X(s) = \dfrac{7}{(s^2+16)(s^2+49)}</m>, which is exactly <xref ref="ex-4-2-4"/>.
        Therefore
        <me>x(t) = \frac{7}{132}\sin(4t) - \frac{1}{33}\sin(7t).</me>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q3: Forced oscillator, nonzero ICs
       (inverse transform of the PFD piece done in ex-4-2-3)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-4-3-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Laplace transforms to solve the following initial value problem.
        <me>x'' + x = 2\cos(3t), \qquad x(0) = 1, \quad x'(0) = 0</me>
        After solving for <m>X(s)</m>, one of the two terms is exactly
        <xref ref="ex-4-2-3"/>, and you may use your result from that exercise for it.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Transforming and solving for <m>X(s)</m> gives
        <me>X(s) = \frac{2s}{(s^2+1)(s^2+9)} + \frac{s}{s^2+1}.</me>
        Use <xref ref="ex-4-2-3"/> on the first term; the second term matches
        <xref ref="formula-E" text="custom">Formula E</xref> directly.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Using <xref ref="ex-4-2-3"/> for the first term and
        <xref ref="formula-E" text="custom">Formula E</xref> for the second:
        <md>
          <mrow>x(t) \amp= \left(\frac{1}{4}\cos(t) - \frac{1}{4}\cos(3t)\right) + \cos(t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{5}{4}\cos(t) - \frac{1}{4}\cos(3t).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Q4: Constant forcing, repeated factor in denominator
       (inverse transform done in ex-4-2-2)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-4-3-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Laplace transforms to solve the following initial value problem.
        <me>y'' + y' = 2, \qquad y(0) = 0, \quad y'(0) = 0</me>
        After solving for <m>Y(s)</m> and factoring the denominator, you may use your
        result from <xref ref="ex-4-2-2"/> to finish the problem.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Apply <xref ref="formula-A" text="custom">Formula A</xref> to transform the
        right-hand side. Collecting terms gives <m>(s^2+s)Y(s) = \dfrac{2}{s}</m>;
        factor <m>s^2 + s</m> before dividing.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>
        Transforming gives <m>(s^2+s)Y(s) = \dfrac{2}{s}</m>, so
        <m>Y(s) = \dfrac{2}{s \cdot s(s+1)} = \dfrac{2}{s^2(s+1)}</m>,
        which is exactly <xref ref="ex-4-2-2"/>. Therefore
        <me>y(t) = -2 + 2t + 2e^{-t}.</me>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-4-3-ex" iframe="question-form.html?section=4.3 exercises" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</exercises>
